#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100640 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cad1e6a-c137-573e-8cad-c992931540a6" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Function Given the Graph of its Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "9b188b17-3d3f-5063-b12b-cdc6b58191d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-339" :question/problem "The graph of $y=f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown below. Sketch a possible graph of $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/e3/e3feb58766bbbb4dc2ed0cd738c7a5d74d31a6d3bbc2e210010b9e5ecbc21da8.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$$\nx=-1\\quad \\text{and} \\quad x=1\n$$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If $f'(x) <         0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{not extr.}$ | $\\searrow$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical lines $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-1,1),$ the point at $x=1$ must be lower than the point at $x=-1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/27/27cfed2793c78d0abaa91c4d5480724303ebf729fa22befbd6142e8e54039e64.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-\\infty,-1)$ and decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-1,1)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-1,1)$ and decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(1,\\infty)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and goes down to the right of $x=1.$\n\n- Furthermore, since $f'(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x = 1$, we must have $f''(x) >       0$, and, since it is decreasing to the right of $x = 1$, we must have $f''(x) <    0.$ So, $x =1$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave up to concave down.\n\n![](images/15/15842026cf3d019dcfd31c0f4f9aad54a4c8465dc1464feaa2d07b45e109ef74.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/0f/0f7e45db7621e8ecd7d915d49d14a8656070365023f12d7239c435f7da8f72fc.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100641 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfa3432b-2bf3-51c8-befb-0299783e7111" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Continuous Function Given the Graph of its Non-Smooth Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "dec0e17e-d9c6-5b6e-994f-717fb60b75fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-340" :question/problem "The graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of a continuous function $y=f(x)$, is given below. Sketch a possible graph of $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/23/23c5620bdd59a6359eaa9bad6580b4b844237bead64b7138f7472b65c7e17c1b.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$$\nx=1\\quad \\text{and} \\quad x=2\n$$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If $f'(x) <               0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,2)$ | $2$ | $(2, \\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n\n \n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=2.$ \n\n- We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=1.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(1,2),$ the point at $x=2$ must be lower than the point at $x=1.$\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/e3/e3a15ad7af8061bb800c5536fe054594402c7898ef7485a8a4165aff9cdc272d.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-\\infty,1)$ and decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(1,2)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=1.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(1,2)$ and increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(2,\\infty)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=2.$\n\n- Furthermore, since $f'(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x = 3$, we must have $f''(x) >   0.$ So, the curve $y=f(x)$ is concave up on $(2,3).$ Also, since $f'(x)$ is constant and positive to the right of $x = 3$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ represents a section of an increasing  straight line on $(3,\\infty).$\n\n![](images/20/200f45be73f0c38d5cab4099ac83ab5d80022539e17cd0996f1ab0228a90a89e.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/16/16aff3b702623f3b6339cacfcdac4c9b43900e58f1228481d168b8b0535e1412.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100766 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15bfb4b0-21fc-577d-9d5e-bcd7e06b2be1" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Function Given the Graph of its Derivative and a Point on the Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "42f35d1e-5f81-5616-8d07-6c7bcf42d107" :question/math-academy-id "e-6929" :question/problem "The graph of $y=f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown below. Given that $f(0)=0,$ sketch a possible graph of $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/ef/ef91945c0b2261785677c4c8ba7e6d202f62bc6c2da20a213bcba03cbcad8556.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point:\n\n$$\nx=-2\n$$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If $f'(x) <          0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f'$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. Consider the vertical line $x=-2.$\n\n- Since we are told that $f(0)=0,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-2,\\infty),$ the point at $x=-2$ must be lower than the point at $x=0.$\n\n![](images/4b/4b599481e7a73ec38b6f0f627b1b9a551c65e6cd3875b3789f0e7ebfa03eb786.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical point:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-\\infty,-2)$ and increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-2,\\infty)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=-2.$\n\n![](images/1a/1ae0017fd07d6e9fa04f233ca31ab89a07260a5dbc5557e4c8ae35ff91d61f43.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/c2/c215907aa0cc156e92ab3b6710d844e774df3149656fc2f71f27ee01db87382e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100767 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07cdfef0-85d9-5197-b2f2-2f9052d83a3b" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Continuous Function Given its Derivative in Algebraic Form"}] :question/id #uuid "b1fe80bf-85bd-590f-806b-1d22d818a696" :question/math-academy-id "e-6930" :question/problem "If $f'(x)=|x+1|,$ sketch a possible graph of a continuous function $y=f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=f'(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](images/57/57937f50c8adac57cd0a8b131ae2605466fe5e87e85ba324977a95b2f2c7a006.png)\n\nRecall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\qquad$ $x=-1$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If $f'(x) <      0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{no extr.}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=-1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/29/296d0c0e63bedc37841aa914e0fb07320fff634ea1f6ac019a84cc47b87966b4.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-\\infty,-1)$ and on $(-1,\\infty)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down the left of $x=-1$ and goes up to the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Furthermore, since $f'(x)$ is decreasing to the left of $x = -1$, we must have $f''(x) <      0$, and, since it is increasing to the right of $x = -1$, we must have $f''(x) >    0.$ So, $x =-1$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave down to concave up.\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/1c/1c4fb55a07e1b55448ec2a2ada4fea07f363542cdf04a8676c90938b5c4f7fbe.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/df/dfeb793809943dc496cc1f47b26f9b20aa3403b6d999787a7444ede605547226.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15bfb4b0-21fc-577d-9d5e-bcd7e06b2be1" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Function Given the Graph of its Derivative and a Point on the Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fcb5e11-8265-5d3d-abe8-a79338f84817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b4/b495e7d55739c157dba9e37ba5c994bcbb28cb403a196e300dc58a12a30332f6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517521} {:answer/id #uuid "1ada6148-fd53-5252-bdd5-2e58d5b36350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0f/0f40ecd297d20c736f8c78f7a2cfda99089e8b23ed2ea8b48282a7ee3a095df5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517538} {:answer/id #uuid "608acc0f-3137-57b6-84d3-462f080a438f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/99/99844134ca18d5a71bdb32c4a36c8b40e284d03b58dd33e9cf7dc01f994a270b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517664} {:answer/id #uuid "71bdb9dc-4aa1-502b-aa8c-40453b138c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/58/58a3a702c5a7e25691c05451e0f3e7e33ea6209fb7a74840ae1fd2f1698bd98b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517706} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f4363f-5b8b-52cf-a83a-2bbc43742fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9f/9f4d9c8e8c29c735a98e7849e367f8f2b92283a9ed0ac28f22835ae8e1ebb527.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ada6148-fd53-5252-bdd5-2e58d5b36350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0f/0f40ecd297d20c736f8c78f7a2cfda99089e8b23ed2ea8b48282a7ee3a095df5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517538} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8000223-fc08-5e87-86f4-e925b30d30c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "451559e6-da00-5040-a946-bd30072c74e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-115622" :question/problem "![](images/94/944d7dfa7895e5eecc2d11065e268fceba725c8181ffa8283d1fcac39d2d5a98.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Given that $f(0)=0,$ which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$\\,$ $x=1\\,$ and $\\,x=3.5$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3.5)$ | $3.5$ | $(3.5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=3.5.$ \n\n- Since we are told that $f(0)=0,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,1),$ the point at $x=1$ must be lower than the point at $x=0.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(1,3.5),$ the point at $x=3.5$ must be higher than the point at $x=1.$\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/4e/4e6fcbb39c9750bb41a2b505325a1b11a633ca749d740aea7c6737bee7db7f7a.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,1)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(1,3.5)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=1.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(1,3.5)$ and decreasing $↘$ on $(3.5,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=3.5.$\n\n![](images/c0/c010f5bb4dcee1241211beefa90e9496045f2b5e5ddc861bd703e8d0cf0f3276.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/49/493dd437fa00fe3d3056dcb6c2a0b291db27d8bdf96e96ee5366d696bf9433e2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cad1e6a-c137-573e-8cad-c992931540a6" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Function Given the Graph of its Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c7ec600-722a-5561-b042-87d0c5ff7aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b1/b1dbe2acbf5cea11d7f7ab108d6e1d7207a3377013fb8f58cd8a94094d8b50f4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517514} {:answer/id #uuid "29513485-282f-5496-828f-ff89eed1167f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5d/5db7f225e29d4a40e315315a2ee4ac4a966cb405a48f7dc721386b996e7ac8e5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517561} {:answer/id #uuid "9b410d03-6da5-58e1-8c43-d0843c59cf86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9d/9d2fc0c667694e4ecf6b9dbaa35ac8a43dd6aee411b3a6e4aa3e9f732daf6bd4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517786} {:answer/id #uuid "b5e39dd5-cfb6-58b5-9f45-a03ccb0e3371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b8/b8aaadbeeba478729c99ef44f408489dd82de55e4d75709d25d4c1e7a2da1213.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517831} {:answer/id #uuid "e55e0b29-9cc3-5de5-8242-1ee9c35b3e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f6/f69aa9b32b1a986aa274f34ff5f60c83fb1c230edd8c2be40739a1a798e71a84.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c7ec600-722a-5561-b042-87d0c5ff7aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b1/b1dbe2acbf5cea11d7f7ab108d6e1d7207a3377013fb8f58cd8a94094d8b50f4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517514} :answer-field/id #uuid "8611c52c-2be7-5b1d-974d-d74d462d8be5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06d5e4de-57e7-5985-961d-b5e59bdab913" :question/math-academy-id "q-115653" :question/problem "![](images/28/28a16cda2f97557c23908fcd45928da5d4e11102343c33eaf07d1990fb0b6baf.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=0$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=0,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/ea6ac357860556acba25d5510ff0e65a4b66acc65e035cd218715b0e96807519.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-\\infty ,0)$ and decreasing $↘$ on $(0,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=0.$\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/2a/2aafc298a8a7b0856d339582bb1236e9ae97c728eba103c703998e14455fdde5.png)\n\n \n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/04/04ec8de5abfecc23f3b4fb844b4e725e2a4f94aeb12bef61cda6356a6522826c.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfa3432b-2bf3-51c8-befb-0299783e7111" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Continuous Function Given the Graph of its Non-Smooth Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "202a670c-3269-5f08-a7c3-3b9487b0946a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/41d7b307e397e681741e0e8dcec970910a321f5463faac0b416d342b8816dbc7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517544} {:answer/id #uuid "63d63f05-32fb-537b-8ebf-d31b75ad82aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b1/b19d252539cae91ae4d50be46f90a53eea32de60c0a1f2ab5e044a48484b7f37.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517672} {:answer/id #uuid "97f473a3-9332-5e36-8e77-f568b64c5771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dc/dc67c64ea8255e0c10b85d89015ad7be68260af9bb7eb1a8e5738da230899ea9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517777} {:answer/id #uuid "b86c4088-ed6c-55c5-918a-4070ede987df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/20fb26562589d8a59082444ca5b641fe5f30aee6397cbd3aed0fdd4473ac9834.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517834} {:answer/id #uuid "e60cb952-35e8-5356-be72-1d1afc0ff8f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/62/626cb1436a1c9088246e70624823e87c7314c02fa67c3d7a361cd13abb451c3a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b86c4088-ed6c-55c5-918a-4070ede987df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/20fb26562589d8a59082444ca5b641fe5f30aee6397cbd3aed0fdd4473ac9834.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517834} :answer-field/id #uuid "773c66ea-cbaa-54fe-9a70-61f6661e90d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "140bcd57-110f-51e2-8837-ad085a0b4faa" :question/math-academy-id "q-115938" :question/problem "![](images/02/02bec11dec3fc03b97b918c392969b246e6b2751d78c0b32b796bdeb4e6eaaca.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of a continuous function $y=f(x)$, is given above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point:  \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1$  \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical line $x=-1$ and choose an arbitrary point on it.\n\n$\\,$ So, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/90/9062dcd04f6ca0dcb21453596ebd007ccf91a33f5092a601d24e401e888a20f1.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(-1,\\infty )$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Moreover, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x=2$ and to the right of $x=2$, although with different slopes, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$ So, the curve $y=f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,2)$  and on $(2,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/fb/fbd0da8fbd98e0b83cd1efb82d5e1f1aca1bf40108e11c6a401da46e514a133a.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/95/95cc3ce5bab09a3e3ea32571e2156d13605b85bf8f026c7c35b5dd51d045c7fd.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518142 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07cdfef0-85d9-5197-b2f2-2f9052d83a3b" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Continuous Function Given its Derivative in Algebraic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2501361d-dea7-52d8-95f4-909628b1ca49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1e/1e356ae7a05aed6c957e3f4bc4d0bb9cd3306fde032cdc4fce13b3f2670590f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517557} {:answer/id #uuid "662d296e-2fb6-522b-9d87-8b74a1007e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/01/01ee0bf05b0e8e3e3c42ef77d38a9613b33ef16106fd661646cedf1aead67652.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517676} {:answer/id #uuid "70937625-c7a6-5e1c-afbe-168a45b60842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/25/257a44d0001820f6df4c3039d7fd24b267bca0031140ccfb5fadd791848eb2d1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517704} {:answer/id #uuid "77d8afa2-9300-57e8-9603-62547c09412e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/58/58ca054838929e04cbc16afaf793f46eef9bcbc9e29dc816419467b478ebdc60.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517714} {:answer/id #uuid "9b279c76-6d9a-53b2-819c-9b6329f30287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1c/1ca104b65355875e55dcabee9e2e553c265812eb6b43fdf1b42df17d9590799f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77d8afa2-9300-57e8-9603-62547c09412e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/58/58ca054838929e04cbc16afaf793f46eef9bcbc9e29dc816419467b478ebdc60.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517714} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3499959-bd1a-5110-afef-51a07c10785e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "585b182e-7b17-5dc5-940a-b3dd1c063524" :question/math-academy-id "q-116853" :question/problem "If ${f}^{′}(x)=-|2x-5|$ and $f(1)=0,$ which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function  $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](images/5f/5f48f6278983a74be84b95984808b23745ab4960dbd427f942efe18df86768e8.png)\n\nRecall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2})$ | $2$ | $(\\frac{5}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{no extr.}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. Consider the vertical line  $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$ \n\n- Since we are told that $f(1)=0,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(1,\\frac{5}{2}),$ the point at $x=1$ must be higher than the point at $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\n![](images/1f/1ff02109b863615e5274b9ac41306644d8d42ce62671e0e76ecbda6655d249e0.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2})$ and on $(\\frac{5}{2},\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left of $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ and goes down to the right of $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x=\\frac{5}{2}$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0$, and, since it is decreasing to the right of $x=\\frac{5}{2}$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ So, $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave up to concave down.\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/93/931e7763569df18dd0aea8cd1363f6d8414f1a0cc8deddc964ba11a9c74fe127.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/ad/ad5016295fb3d33baac6af6e74dd3b2cbf563c74aef78bf27e5aed28222db3e7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cad1e6a-c137-573e-8cad-c992931540a6" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Function Given the Graph of its Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c91278e-e513-584a-b5f1-2e83547243af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ad/adb436d22258853f072ad913c72f7aced89ed9bac2e90c64284f5e9fd207d677.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517516} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca7260b-4bd3-578c-99fc-28ba65fbc818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/86/86693cdfeb10911b4c6aa6aa9a2b514a80110a18d63c15af955a61fc42011b15.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517572} {:answer/id #uuid "bb73b6eb-a369-59a9-938a-33e3e19e8180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c4/c4685eebacdd1401c818e9d0f1af403d3f2bcc8187c5a7044c5e8ed2970320bc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517843} {:answer/id #uuid "cd05fdf8-a19d-5595-9827-470daf59e904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ff/ff4a407ff40e277e8730996778531bd6b8541332340b1c7bcc1abfdb3c55faf2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517873} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ad64e9-bb9a-5050-bf3c-1c8d2b65fefe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b0/b0a49b1b88d6bdf4a45598ef6fe1550b6c8abfdc8d7000d4f34ce174ed9a3544.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c91278e-e513-584a-b5f1-2e83547243af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ad/adb436d22258853f072ad913c72f7aced89ed9bac2e90c64284f5e9fd207d677.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517516} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba5c71aa-5435-51b8-bfad-c3e21dfbafbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01274ce5-42e5-5d91-b593-1351d7ffb44f" :question/math-academy-id "q-20507" :question/problem "![](images/2d/2dd50d4970f5c738c7cc350b8aee4668a6ec9ce4b1444698c5ecd737d01f82ea.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-2$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=-2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9eae519cd7ca99aa85fe62c9fa8276a7455cbc869587296405c6a6b3caf2ee30.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(-2,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and the right of $x=-2.$\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/1a/1a2fd6311c5845885bfd7ed9118091a5c81c4fea8c81587db79b203a51b9e1de.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/f6/f6e473044a84d215de98e3739bea83ccaf107231ccb273c8e45db91221f05edb.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cad1e6a-c137-573e-8cad-c992931540a6" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Function Given the Graph of its Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d2eb6d5-ddb8-5d23-875a-5e2002482d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d2/d25d30efea96fd63f923da67668ef77b7af69701fb3ef34497c66009dc1be031.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099170} {:answer/id #uuid "d811e88f-4ab8-5afc-a0ed-485346467342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/42/42d7fe32912cf573862dcf1aaf191706efb7ff3188436281f67f6c49912e471f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099171} {:answer/id #uuid "0e6ea1c4-f91e-5f11-99d9-8f330ee938ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fa/fa6b7fa93e98c2d641a8026b6a4dc1a7d17c1c909da270deef29d63e64d0f5a4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099172} {:answer/id #uuid "22fdd574-fe48-5460-bbcd-08be7a378a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ed/edad2e1d26b98973d4a888058da155510b31f009759db7f2b6707dc4bf5a6c50.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099173} {:answer/id #uuid "3375239b-8f1e-5ebc-9151-0f79d24d5dd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bf9e9f8fa1d2bc0ec7036870102cdd50de260fa2aff23af3cbcf2678018f12aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d811e88f-4ab8-5afc-a0ed-485346467342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/42/42d7fe32912cf573862dcf1aaf191706efb7ff3188436281f67f6c49912e471f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099171} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffe4290b-772c-5d6f-ba6e-07cc2478306b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "805cd960-0e6c-5413-ba15-d13411a05c8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-20510" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e1d4dbd07dbf4e7f06b5ef2dd01ae2ef5fa82f3a5a78fb470388fad8b18af1e.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1.5$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1.5)$ | $-1.5$ | $(-1.5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{no extr.}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=-1.5,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/8b/8bee8a231cdfe8d41cf35fa50d97806d511a9477a8c92db98b1be11b70676928.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-\\infty ,-1.5)$ and is increasing $↗$ on $(-1.5,+\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and up to the right of $x=-1.5.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing to the left of $x=-1.5$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0$, and, since it is increasing to the right of $x=-1.5$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$ So, $x=-1.5$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave down to concave up.\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/89/89127ba4db33c1c2ca12ddac05eac1c7e5ebe6540519fda1b55600620c1369d7.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/20/20784e268300c461c4567092230df10423b96d26287fde67074bab6690c1342f.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cad1e6a-c137-573e-8cad-c992931540a6" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Function Given the Graph of its Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d03b9e5-c929-51d2-935e-306438fa83a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/85/85966acfdc882b9f4d5a74ca6c058234e8522e164adf90392a9a2fd6407d1e2d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517695} {:answer/id #uuid "c63bf268-63be-5348-a926-f6543d453618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/94/94aa7db16724e58ee9154a647939dad7c31270c6a7c933b0b5616f20b20dd358.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517865} {:answer/id #uuid "cb162731-bef9-5c76-993a-ea53fb58aec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1c/1cedc81848d9a334a782ab8912849b883e0f7c346aa5c22dc9fffb549e6da2e9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517871} {:answer/id #uuid "d72461d3-54b5-5e48-b328-5f7605c2e405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b9/b92b3f9451964d3c17cca0fffa9d378a3d40d8e8a1cbf3a2da1373c75558de94.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517896} {:answer/id #uuid "f19c0551-6cba-54d6-939e-c1754d401a58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/08/08023ee8e2c3794d6c387e8484ce30477af40883e13c244eaeb53dd410f1d7d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c63bf268-63be-5348-a926-f6543d453618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/94/94aa7db16724e58ee9154a647939dad7c31270c6a7c933b0b5616f20b20dd358.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517865} :answer-field/id #uuid "097df743-cf70-55f0-86fa-ee810d941deb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e89c21de-ead1-56e3-b348-ff522b66f5d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-20519" :question/problem "![](images/a5/a53074660204fdd4fd62575ef8db80a3b04144ef7b9e18d98c60fbbe9a480ee3.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1\\,$ and $\\,x=1$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical lines $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-1,1),$ the point at $x=1$ must be higher than the point at $x=-1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/12/12e6a8036759b8bbcc2f4fbe4ae7f4f7e18278de65fbdc0faa99c919f7234284.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and is increasing $↗$ on $(-1,1)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-1,1)$ and is decreasing $↘$ on $(1,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and the right of $x=1.$\n\n![](images/38/38f1c92810a3311ed939a54ac2856cb7ada111dbf24528785f80bd5f9ca49c84.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/79/7921a107b33a9b9bdc3df8dec418828f4cd5de2d89a9e7ab41845b0c64bbb653.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15bfb4b0-21fc-577d-9d5e-bcd7e06b2be1" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Function Given the Graph of its Derivative and a Point on the Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b480465-5f89-5451-9e32-2c7626bd187f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/df/dfe39efab675842e00c2cfe764ea91579fc6cc908150bfc92b0e6059861151fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517511} {:answer/id #uuid "19e34821-b31b-56fb-abfd-64b1ae126d7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/aa/aa84e4e1164f08ed09630ab29d3ea58ac64bae2fb477ca52bd877fcc68446749.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517535} {:answer/id #uuid "21cab66b-5fb3-57ce-b282-ad7beb64df5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7d/7dd91e0d84d8f5074442ad15b81c7edd9d42542fd44bda2e92c7de51191ffd0b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517551} {:answer/id #uuid "31788703-390d-516a-b9de-d18025f61121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a4/a4dd5fe6f307cfb3f710aecf0d1ec7c15d1d40f877859a4924fe763427ddee75.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517583} {:answer/id #uuid "cdd55c45-aef4-5eef-86a3-efc38346e0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/53/530947fa1d6069fcdd8f8c9659d1150f78c124981dc3229317d5725108ecb07e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21cab66b-5fb3-57ce-b282-ad7beb64df5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7d/7dd91e0d84d8f5074442ad15b81c7edd9d42542fd44bda2e92c7de51191ffd0b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517551} :answer-field/id #uuid "9574a838-800d-5813-a9de-cbd6d28e1a98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e28fed1-97b3-526c-8da9-ef22fe1e5ddd" :question/math-academy-id "q-20520" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3c304ff10a466abe8a7a98342f58d357fec5a46c6971c5dc0b30203bbbb1ec62.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Given that $f(0)=0,$ which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-0.5$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-0.5)$ | $-0.5$ | $(-0.5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. Consider the vertical lines $x=-0.5.$ \n\n- Since we are told that $f(0)=0,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-0.5,\\infty ),$ the point at $x=-0.5$ must be higher than the point at $x=0.$\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/9a/9a90c6cc7702720da4fb2619935ce1f663fe09a1f0827c520371d4dc39881cf5.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical point:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-\\infty ,-0.5)$ and decreasing $↘$ on $(-0.5,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=-0.5.$\n\n![](images/02/02833ae5c4579ec492812be7bc8e90958108a60cc2f5b7348d4eb6119f667062.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/8f/8ffc84aff1ce37e77b8c0c169a6cb01b9bff7f5f2d27a834ccea251a7d1acbde.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfa3432b-2bf3-51c8-befb-0299783e7111" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Continuous Function Given the Graph of its Non-Smooth Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b7ce8cd-7ab6-5f76-a77d-c0ee883c9a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d59a89bbbf7a9e61c6e273e86838005e57db02c5b779ea08dcc9f651c1f990b6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099176} {:answer/id #uuid "8ec993fa-d510-57df-84a3-f00d841a108b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/88/88d0da37eb66c3ed550597fb025ec53f5831d8363eb7010a59b69e5692edb65b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099177} {:answer/id #uuid "458a0549-a881-523a-bb3e-138c0bce5813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/575aca42b8b76a18f493d0d0bcfe8d971221a519f90046a9b80c9dd9b2326fb8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099178} {:answer/id #uuid "65da5940-b674-595a-b182-7f40863ad996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/98/987c5d64b39ca266b7db61f341d62288b108bb0acbb10c5621b4d5b6927be299.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099179} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0e4264-cffb-5dad-a2bd-e659da0b9e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d5deaa53bc174ee1c3f0f920ec981abb9add793f3c684d3dc2c2bd59066ac3e5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "458a0549-a881-523a-bb3e-138c0bce5813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/575aca42b8b76a18f493d0d0bcfe8d971221a519f90046a9b80c9dd9b2326fb8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099178} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd32a449-17a6-5e19-a118-15b84895542f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72e69ce6-788b-5045-a97d-e5fb2ac0d0b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-20522" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c1b92084149ea54a3d309abf1a441c0f6855b15dc999f18c0108b02130d97ce8.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of a continuous function $y=f(x)$, is given above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$\\,$ $x=0\\,$ and $\\,x=1$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\n- We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=0.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(0,1),$ the point at $x=1$ must be lower than the point at $x=0.$\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/db/db3e0263e727e75197468f970ac85bdde2a2a9cdfb9257db0809665112772095.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-\\infty ,0)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(0,1)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=0.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↗$ on $(0,1)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(1,\\infty )$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=1.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is constant to the left of $x=0$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ represents a section of a straight line on $(-\\infty ,0).$ Also, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the right of $x=0$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$ So, the curve $y=f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/d8/d8cf7b97d4e9356b275b669d59b0498286bc673d55fbc533891daac369d5cd35.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/5f/5fc6864d1b7530ecd4fa1b151390b85d32a5d0628d0f51160cfaab7fc61e33b9.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15bfb4b0-21fc-577d-9d5e-bcd7e06b2be1" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Function Given the Graph of its Derivative and a Point on the Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172bf440-f574-5070-8f2d-33edad832211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b4/b48f710984436c4a30f39a9206179c8b1291ab68b20c0ad46eaa3f453bcea469.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517529} {:answer/id #uuid "4845549a-32c4-5e66-b53c-55d09aa341e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a0/a061eb4bccd90e77ed8f5ed546575f1d8693b5e5d9664dd25e38db736af7f9f5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517628} {:answer/id #uuid "acd52841-b68b-50a7-9877-3a6316ab884d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/88/888fdd7918b5c3da97448ca243f3d57a7091ea0370331a919457ecfdb2b3bb5a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517810} {:answer/id #uuid "d971d8a5-7e38-51ca-9066-16ed46c0fd13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/db/dbc432d3951384c6cd50c842fb9d8da09b77724fb932cac0310696496c0e05da.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517902} {:answer/id #uuid "edc7ce5c-ea51-542b-bd83-b0c6b0e2ffe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f9/f9735d4f15740958dba71e285cb59aefed45e6964e82f040c7560880760f09ea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acd52841-b68b-50a7-9877-3a6316ab884d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/88/888fdd7918b5c3da97448ca243f3d57a7091ea0370331a919457ecfdb2b3bb5a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517810} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b860c17-9b4c-57c9-b76d-64ac14b97c60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b66ab71-e5ba-5e8c-8d29-3a56ef93e008" :question/math-academy-id "q-20536" :question/problem "![](images/3d/3d1a01139fdd6f0f9f996cfce43a1e5ad86c2eabe526e9501f72451bd137f9dd.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Given that $f(0)=0,$ which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$\\,$ $x=0\\,$ and $\\,x=1$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ \n\n- Since we are told that $f(0)=0,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(0,1),$ the point at $x=1$ must be higher than the point at $x=0.$\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/cf/cfd74974fecdaf62309e089f34f99e0f015584ed3fbb275b9b997c9811c74979.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,0)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(0,1)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=0.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(0,1)$ and decreasing $↘$ on $(1,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=1.$\n\n![](images/8f/8faca7d91b813d8ce7a964614a19f29d7e3b20d34cf918463151f37bc8f9614b.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/41/4127d1311f92f1e03def285cf922bd5235fd4b2a5ca9614f34e7243c9bf79276.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15bfb4b0-21fc-577d-9d5e-bcd7e06b2be1" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Function Given the Graph of its Derivative and a Point on the Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ae1d63b-23bd-5309-8ee8-17b745973c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7d/7d5111c260bea478b9b357669ebfc2fe04ba159e2cebbf5f8cea0439ed8adc74.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517509} {:answer/id #uuid "181657a5-8e49-5d1d-999a-0fb0c3f63c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/63/63127d91f491c9bdad7da5a392cfb0393b031e569b034053c20ddb5da27d6b72.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517532} {:answer/id #uuid "2c119c65-b1d1-5c58-9553-3ad2dae34000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/59/59f73f5b191f642dbcd20cacfa960d2a013ac30a1bc2497135a68732eccbde16.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517569} {:answer/id #uuid "df1de746-594f-5aab-bc64-d7589cef3144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ce/ce45a5f05e6d9095c27b5a51cafa9cb0bafd80687d32653c2fa75c9e0b21ef2f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517908} {:answer/id #uuid "fac7db90-a675-5558-8d1a-77b845711d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/94/9415a29205a3c6048a0bdfcf7c6472a03cbaca9342612a0c854c965ccdd0d9ee.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fac7db90-a675-5558-8d1a-77b845711d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/94/9415a29205a3c6048a0bdfcf7c6472a03cbaca9342612a0c854c965ccdd0d9ee.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517960} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c5edabf-00e6-5fe8-8d29-4f1176381b61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1fbb657d-32e1-57fb-9710-afc011b6ab8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-20537" :question/problem "![](images/56/562596b4267c6a13ef13c4f20e7861b00675e85febc43653efd141d720900804.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Given that $f(0)=0,$ which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1\\,$ and $\\,x=0.$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{not extr.}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical lines $x=-1,$ and $x=0.$\n\n- Since we are told that $f(0)=0,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-1,0)$, the point at $x=0$ must be lower than the point at $x=-1.$\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/50/504a1af2c6d97200a3b211e8a7c67298082cd3cffbf866851afa63f7162dcce3.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and on $(-1,0)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and down to the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-1,0)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(0,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=0.$\n\n![](images/6e/6e6c1b9806b3dbf340255884722429a67dc3a7cd55d8cc0a0bbeeb48dcae1c87.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/10/10185719d14812cd4f58a592f106fe4fd81540ab6f6177f61d77bd6ec4e5d6c4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15bfb4b0-21fc-577d-9d5e-bcd7e06b2be1" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Function Given the Graph of its Derivative and a Point on the Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cd26d16-0db1-5668-8fc3-46d952aa6916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5d/5daf580ac012c426dcd74c0ced697a8d8a3a109b3e9762e3315cde514777af40.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517573} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1d4203-e4eb-5d8c-b257-0e8af5b10bab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/40/403d9fea4b302c5f198c64c1be9f6105c3d095a929c4a4f5763b6d35bd350cea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517696} {:answer/id #uuid "6d69587a-0fa0-5c37-b3be-9a8ba46198a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/42/42b75cc427f3a1b1298d1058828ab2808b1df43c3140acfc50dc56380cf0d50d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517698} {:answer/id #uuid "91f31f58-f5b7-5fe0-bf72-8fc0e8e1523d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0e/0e309172615a213d2b8cc54b8efd73f9681e90c7949577939e382fbf1c6e9cbb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517762} {:answer/id #uuid "e9425c54-4f77-5814-a554-2239b7b79e9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3d/3d1b8a4f32a73ac96528ebb09dd68b312b3f50ec5b7b46a0d4c2cfb3052c1d72.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91f31f58-f5b7-5fe0-bf72-8fc0e8e1523d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0e/0e309172615a213d2b8cc54b8efd73f9681e90c7949577939e382fbf1c6e9cbb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517762} :answer-field/id #uuid "91678777-7e28-5ef5-a238-81845caf3318" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7bca001f-f1ef-5c7b-838d-dca97d1b4371" :question/math-academy-id "q-20538" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef23af714377518200c574ba436a30001ed5f60262e11f8157f62d49cbec317e.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Given that $f(0)=0,$ which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are three critical points: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-3,$ $x=-2,\\,$ and $\\,x=-1.$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical lines $x=-3,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=-1.$\n\n- Since we are told that $f(0)=0,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-1,0)$, the point at $x=-1$ must be below the $x$-axis.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-2,-1)$, the point at $x=-2$ must be higher than the point at $x=-1.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-3,-2)$, the point at $x=-3$ must be lower than the point at $x=-2.$\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/66/66296cbca22d3059fba111935808d1bb0a78e9de31c45948fce207d998c9cfd2.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(-3,-2)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=-3.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-3,-2)$ and decreasing $↘$ on $(-2,-1)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=-2.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-2,-1)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(-1,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=-1.$\n\n![](images/b2/b26af4cdc5af02f2cf0a9a0092f0fe908d70acd428e6a1e24bc848bcc99125e4.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/93/938c86a415620a2e80873f7cee72b2ebaf5579ae2e67eb966bb1f8836e62c087.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07cdfef0-85d9-5197-b2f2-2f9052d83a3b" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Continuous Function Given its Derivative in Algebraic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82ae8a1f-7dd0-5150-8920-a28e6297e634" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f4/f46e345eb290a9225411babcb08ebc3ad24083c54c92eb81c7e77271e3fc19d1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099832} {:answer/id #uuid "9c8de85c-3369-5866-99fd-3ed2137b5dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3f/3f3920952d7961cede9e3c0da4c2cb41d31bcdd82942e66286b2d2409baaa750.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099833} {:answer/id #uuid "564c7134-e7be-5de4-b0bb-eda0fd78c198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/59/59b3496a7e4583ca9af9318a6363578fc755fe6e89604a14ad46eb565f9a1468.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099834} {:answer/id #uuid "620a4bb4-30cf-50ba-901b-a34b6f31b8d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/86/86959eec84fd8a9e02d6ce5c0715fb7c4a43621692b1a4058af75341eb00c6e7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099835} {:answer/id #uuid "625f7386-2031-5cbb-96dd-bcaf1a7d45d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e0/e0cd3e8e31f46fffc3b9e7060c5f1d0c9e637fd86a097093caa3f64f26edeff6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c8de85c-3369-5866-99fd-3ed2137b5dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3f/3f3920952d7961cede9e3c0da4c2cb41d31bcdd82942e66286b2d2409baaa750.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099833} :answer-field/id #uuid "28df9e23-f800-52c9-9c9c-1069ec9448fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0894de1-f2ec-5434-b75f-84e94baff54e" :question/math-academy-id "q-49036" :question/problem "If ${f}^{′}(x)=-|x+2|,$ which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](images/0b/0bfc917724b8339ef52888f287f2bc1c69fca41e9207f42ff3d83a633fe3cc0a.png)\n\nRecall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-2$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{no extr.}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=-2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d032eca4d1a29d5a7809dfd5c43cce52b8a0b66f1c8bdc9b9747cf704da524f5.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and is decreasing $↘$ on $(-2,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left of $x=-2$ and goes down to the right of $x=-2.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x=-2$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0$, and, since it is decreasing to the right of $x=-2$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ So, $x=-2$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave up to concave down.\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/ba/ba965ae91587d92beee6b5acb9af809167bb1de01fe1c2cdb8b0637244356e7f.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/fb/fbe2e07c3c82e1140e2e9d687020ece8dad208251dfeec2bddbd27bc5c44841d.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07cdfef0-85d9-5197-b2f2-2f9052d83a3b" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Continuous Function Given its Derivative in Algebraic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07719b01-252d-561b-87e2-85e6097f49cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/52/526256c33ec7cc7dd4d2e66ac743ee4ec5f77cd6611be0058b803c29259824fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517504} {:answer/id #uuid "5f90d7a0-fc50-5df6-bd19-a6dbc4b8c478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b7/b740045fb77d5e41f92f008ebfd17a29758ca79a0c6711cc9085d58928ec11d1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517663} {:answer/id #uuid "610df0ff-02a5-5fd9-b2d8-32a935126b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fb/fb8dd043b5f55a1626e5a9856b6e59ef064d0ea73e28bab78ab04f38811b6ba1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517667} {:answer/id #uuid "806f2f90-f4e8-5f10-b45c-0d7aaaa1c145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/68/684a49f4159946cf99aabca94aabeb8ff51f5913364e4f463e0922ec13f1ffa2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517731} {:answer/id #uuid "bcdbe134-8973-5cf5-b776-513ac4d24b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e9/e9cd32a7123e1b31ed920f5df4455fc66a69cb6ea38ae0d2a19d2ed1acf6daa3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07719b01-252d-561b-87e2-85e6097f49cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/52/526256c33ec7cc7dd4d2e66ac743ee4ec5f77cd6611be0058b803c29259824fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517504} :answer-field/id #uuid "12fe7014-9a7c-5cd6-8814-4c6fc23c44c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0bf75373-8a0d-58e6-9585-31096e6c716e" :question/math-academy-id "q-49040" :question/problem "If ${f}^{′}(x)=|x+5|,$ which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](images/ae/ae96c488c78fb207567c6ae0ea7497782af1e840a7852bd79b57902d45972a01.png)\n\nRecall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-5$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $-5$ | $(-5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{no extr.}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=-5,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/2c/2c67c136914eba7687d96a66e737e2f1f7d96144d4535b25f0ac48e16aed8396.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-\\infty ,-5)$ and on $(-5,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down to the left of $x=-5$ and goes up to the right of $x=-5.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing to the left of $x=-5$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0$, and, since it is increasing to the right of $x=-5$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$ So, $x=-5$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave down to concave up.\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/97/971e495bbe1d7c502a1e65ec2b0764f0057158e3fe989261245382223020e872.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/00/00320e4c614d3ba3051ffca74a005113b057668488c12d50c5c497d613ce7492.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfa3432b-2bf3-51c8-befb-0299783e7111" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Continuous Function Given the Graph of its Non-Smooth Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbe47f53-5582-51da-883d-303611b6a979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f0/f042b8e120a91b914c7328ff0353ee5f7aabb33e131d2f3da1e5415e3438f8b6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100036} {:answer/id #uuid "d9fcc64e-794e-5cc1-aed5-874d5c44bdb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/38/384294a05bc6580a9b1a963d164e538a20930a94a0855d82e48628b7e1805ebb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100037} {:answer/id #uuid "e7901d6e-90b0-533a-b924-563b619bc7a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/09/0954153f8aab1b5e08fe1ed4c41ae06331bedc3069b8c8cee0afcb2aae5461bc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100038} {:answer/id #uuid "09132355-f0d3-5ab2-a60a-009b69e17e98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f8/f8537359156c71c484801edee852476f0a67a67d84872d3e3d77fd0c2526761d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100039} {:answer/id #uuid "3549dac9-6168-561f-967d-c8c6f0ff3ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/40/408847a845176f67203c35d87932171b2f35150d07403eea22f300087669d31e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7901d6e-90b0-533a-b924-563b619bc7a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/09/0954153f8aab1b5e08fe1ed4c41ae06331bedc3069b8c8cee0afcb2aae5461bc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100038} :answer-field/id #uuid "a19851d6-574f-5fd8-aa19-b650471cb457" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bea5ecd5-1bd7-56d0-b590-dc8806f75f22" :question/math-academy-id "q-51323" :question/problem "![](images/58/58ebecdd8284dff6e807465f29c890bd87ec763896242cbff2bd1d18a4728f49.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of a continuous function $y=f(x)$, is given above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-2\\,$ and $\\,x=3$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical lines $x=-2$ and $x=3.$\n\n- We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=-2.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-2,3),$ the point at $x=3$ must be higher than the point at $x=-2.$\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/ac/ac3ad854bdef4e5c7011a81a14da722cef44657e8024a39533179513589d0867.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(-2,3)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=-2.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(-2,3)$ and decreasing $↘$ on $(3,\\infty )$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=3.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x=1$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0$, and, since it is decreasing to the right of $x=1$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ So, $x=1$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave up to concave down.\n\n![](images/81/81870d6d4fbba512d353788dd06062e380baeb672002f150b2c54eeac73a1bd7.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/b2/b243c1cfb9da30100aee5f215e02c5eb11769cef1ba4b2b5b74f2879e8a34e10.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfa3432b-2bf3-51c8-befb-0299783e7111" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Continuous Function Given the Graph of its Non-Smooth Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ccebf8-2372-5729-987f-d8c7e4cd9f4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/61/6172cc5efc4c98b46e65b28034b608c11f9a8134c6629d61d4f5ae83564cce92.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517595} {:answer/id #uuid "acf0dc42-96ed-5eea-a990-8942bdf1b517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/26/264947554c6cf3b2bbd0a4ab2497d19eb054a4bb7ef0abd8621d5e3372940b5c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517811} {:answer/id #uuid "ce906917-bf5a-5839-b638-64b88832140a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/70/70ced59275e3c88d236ff5b8306f81b008aa64f27d50c5ce2d11598f391034e9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517876} {:answer/id #uuid "e9b89159-d512-5d5f-9dd1-51c2c1223dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b1/b1718dd87304c78cf4ac3899dff470b06d16b894f03921eaf5d34cf8b9e1f092.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517930} {:answer/id #uuid "f6fc7a06-065f-5843-9a83-b37d930e76fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e1/e1b70069bc39b663886252e4e56e82bc330898ef13dfb0112e558f47d6673c4c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36ccebf8-2372-5729-987f-d8c7e4cd9f4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/61/6172cc5efc4c98b46e65b28034b608c11f9a8134c6629d61d4f5ae83564cce92.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517595} :answer-field/id #uuid "487f5468-055d-55b0-8c96-d71409d37900" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d823907c-c12e-50eb-a396-a258624fbd1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-51330" :question/problem "![](images/9e/9e7c8d7f80211aebbd8a8fb5456e2bfe0a5dd0dc46addfe65777eb09f0b4c66a.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of a continuous function $y=f(x)$, is given above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point:  \n\n$\\,$ $x=3$  \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. Consider the vertical lines $x=3$ and choose an arbitrary point on it.\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/4a/4aa4592a6edae5207c7e934aa148e87dd8a8762f6bbdba260cc137bd64b407b9.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $↗$ on $(2,3)$ and decreasing $↘$ on $(3,\\infty )$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=3.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is a positive constant to the left of $x=2$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ represents a section of an increasing  straight line on $(-\\infty ,2).$ Also, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing to the right of $x=2$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ So, the curve $y=f(x)$ is concave down on $(2,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/34/347dda820c8307984b88ffb5f2b528c20984ec8a7e9acbe9b7f3d8f457a917b2.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/99/99de5224b614ea11b454020d7a165b58ff0fcf47708faba9973255276133eebc.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfa3432b-2bf3-51c8-befb-0299783e7111" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Continuous Function Given the Graph of its Non-Smooth Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38488cb0-1ec7-5a4b-a616-33b0ca9ac40a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5c/5cab7678c86c76fffbbc4c9bb851b5fedda6d0e9e49d81bb7fb073fc7fdfe05c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517599} {:answer/id #uuid "4b407a60-9ac1-527a-af7c-51a98f704415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b2/b21b46ac542bbe3dad5074116ecb35eb269606723e714e22a8657b2f61c99252.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517635} {:answer/id #uuid "7e8c7665-fc3c-5a68-8a99-fa28eed4ff40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/72/727d15daa2f28bcc0f22e2e1d3d2ef787b6eb62b32b381a62d89f1103f296ce9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517727} {:answer/id #uuid "b2aa3e73-5cb8-5796-b77f-f356b3ff0ee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7c/7c64b0683d9795ca8c3f76163e2d442d50f11f4735e32a43ab99dbec5707d79c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517823} {:answer/id #uuid "cf830141-9ff6-5e11-a170-2bf7c88ce651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c98e84cf8d461815dc7579d9ee154b4536b41895d67a96c2014b03e9eaff901c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b407a60-9ac1-527a-af7c-51a98f704415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b2/b21b46ac542bbe3dad5074116ecb35eb269606723e714e22a8657b2f61c99252.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517635} :answer-field/id #uuid "38894a8b-e33a-540b-9475-aa15c26c72f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02de7b14-03bb-5d7e-b753-b48cb91786a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-81019" :question/problem "![](images/2a/2ab398982e30d4b0ce633b77541596f3f5848e1b0a25539037ec3ee0fc557d4d.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of a continuous function $y=f(x)$, is given above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point:  \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1.$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\n \n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. Consider the vertical line $x=-1$ and choose an arbitrary point on it.\n\nSo, we draw it as follows:\n\n![](images/77/7719107149a10c6e5a22e6c343b532f3580582ebd78d3667aad5f2684d2f219d.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(-1,0)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x=0$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$ So, the curve $y=f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0).$ Also, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is constant and positive  to the right of $x=0$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ represents a section of an increasing straight line on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/d8/d81008f3d06f41f53a389fa2f36a7b12845fa3dae5a2707a739bbb500c8b4e56.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/76/763163652b41a7b3cc0b7a6b3faca4799cc073238a50afd9bd3d9c7767c32793.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07cdfef0-85d9-5197-b2f2-2f9052d83a3b" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Continuous Function Given its Derivative in Algebraic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56465daf-a408-5f82-8ea8-358730019601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9f/9f546deb760f9e18da71971d3fa3c01b9dd6897c4c60061823c85bb0783d2552.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517650} {:answer/id #uuid "67c380bc-a173-5d17-8537-696cff633db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/10/10e27a1d89f89773c96bf650876c2af9bdfc0bae9227f447d9b9e442c4721341.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517678} {:answer/id #uuid "8b7211ad-1827-5211-9917-bc77faeb57bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/71/71e5c149ba535cd953324c90c6167182b3a16ac71fba585ae281280e7448f63f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517750} {:answer/id #uuid "9a1d6226-8e44-5fa3-8504-f9d5ed1894cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e2/e2c2ae047b8f684f61247d6e6ffdf9ae7207383af5e5dd97c485a1e69b0daa89.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517781} {:answer/id #uuid "c70d4e54-a97f-5592-aa11-ebf05d4c3575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1e/1eca2e86f84ac8fd092d8a45fbf64d0967ec90510716c1cd956f4ae1908206fb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67c380bc-a173-5d17-8537-696cff633db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/10/10e27a1d89f89773c96bf650876c2af9bdfc0bae9227f447d9b9e442c4721341.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517678} :answer-field/id #uuid "435e0da2-147f-55f1-880b-a1dae804184c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "67f4106c-b68b-5844-893c-27d541fb64bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-81038" :question/problem "If ${f}^{′}(x)=-|x-5|,$ which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](images/54/54c03ba5ea869394d9b3d35c64bb012a8c5f9917a5ebb96fd67994ace6a84d4f.png)\n\nRecall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=5$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,5)$ | $5$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{no extr.}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=5,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/25/25d067dcff3b0cc4b88859a318023288a7c9786b5c73910f2addf3952680596b.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,5)$ and on $(5,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left of $x=5$ and goes down to the right of $x=5.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x=5$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0$, and, since it is decreasing to the right of $x=5$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ So, $x=5$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave up to concave down.\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/b7/b7b1e5915931501d881ba5efc1aaccb8af3a604ac397ac662d8bb7811659c17a.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/cb/cb451ce921bb8bc56576545b4503bf3dd44bb1ee070466576437c2ce980dd017.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cad1e6a-c137-573e-8cad-c992931540a6" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Function Given the Graph of its Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34a3e543-5ad8-57bf-9b65-7b2fe2a6b72b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3a/3a136fb82417c4c5425a3f674b0a359b3c5634c20cff3450256b6230a8c23c31.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517590} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2835ab-9301-5c8b-8281-9c2e76030cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/74/743b0c2339d184cccb45f8dec6d615b68ad4c0d985f79971407136c8bb3df175.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517788} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0dd751-de56-55e1-9244-4239a0b555ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/09/093ef67145200dd417bd3405673720bacd4be904c7c9daf7aa6ba953570bfbec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517842} {:answer/id #uuid "eae733fe-f37a-5c46-8b3b-e9f3c17aa8e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/09/09af4beb07576d820f6c4e88ffdde857deb7929c7389e1e2c7cf9a3f722e81d0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517935} {:answer/id #uuid "fb85d946-cf7e-5790-986b-7eb63faffc01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0e/0e1d8e8f6ca871700f677fb51195bdc80a0fdeee0d90a265f8249fa4240223b3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eae733fe-f37a-5c46-8b3b-e9f3c17aa8e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/09/09af4beb07576d820f6c4e88ffdde857deb7929c7389e1e2c7cf9a3f722e81d0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517935} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf339733-37be-5896-bcb1-f51dffe96660" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f23ce06-592e-5661-a235-01cdcf7c430f" :question/math-academy-id "q-86308" :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab5fb36385ed57a83a8bef13b0e58745fef3454d4e3fae0c54491d80b61637ce.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=3$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=3,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/7e/7eb820e9dedb816318045df9f8dbef2d4b3007446599e56414a803f277acd622.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,3)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(3,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and the right of $x=3.$\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/7d/7dd5014441f5cbe3ba3ee0127feb9cea1f8d264255e1c907ce6d5fdc458aa77f.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/fb/fb709866c18a2ecd61160330bd54c44b481a311e3a1bec589ef26ab8a4c6243f.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07cdfef0-85d9-5197-b2f2-2f9052d83a3b" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Continuous Function Given its Derivative in Algebraic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29b1ea0c-801b-5be1-a08e-fdb6f8285ec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/32/322c3c53bd12d39622f10afd832e474dafe5727d7efdfa02ca27a8180fdb1e21.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517564} {:answer/id #uuid "3de6f621-4b21-5871-a21a-bfd5edc277b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4f/4ff204021926fb8140deac869598a0b2d07af7aa713be3b86c038eee20762787.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517612} {:answer/id #uuid "50c8ca44-7181-5697-b043-5a4ebdd0c813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cc/cca7ac0dff44446267f01c7097816aa16bc9e649c01b04697903a39d3954fbfa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517646} {:answer/id #uuid "685fcd83-1689-59db-af7a-bdad5315d17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/79/79898d06aa308ba19ea4877cc43660ef4dbce8f23fa6b22c70973338016591de.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517680} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e0f135-edca-5fd3-bd5e-c030bd5dcea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/42/420902d226813d062cf466223eca8b8f6eb5f38d664784c91d02e5b0c588013a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29b1ea0c-801b-5be1-a08e-fdb6f8285ec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/32/322c3c53bd12d39622f10afd832e474dafe5727d7efdfa02ca27a8180fdb1e21.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517564} :answer-field/id #uuid "df53f24c-911c-54cb-8674-87e3cac5cc46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9fc26721-45c6-5872-bde4-819ab63691ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-86431" :question/problem "If ${f}^{′}(x)=-|x+1|,$ which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](images/c4/c4e03ef210691e4eee922d0c35e90abb9cde1a78064ad491d4ac084d875c4a57.png)\n\nRecall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{no extr.}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=-1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/40/40b5e9b8928be63a415877c0633f0302c874db51b1c74a1f82b7b1526a6f4d14.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and is decreasing $↘$ on $(-1,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left of $x=-1$ and goes down to the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x=-1$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0$, and, since it is decreasing to the right of $x=-1$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ So, $x=-1$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave up to concave down.\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](images/c6/c68544957f33afdeab436a3fbf75b9e27990a2e7f3ea75f39c6e58dce24f6185.png)\n\n \n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/4c/4c91ba8f06642fe14035400efa61dbd2d8bbadd07274fedc9a90678970bbb9a8.png)\n\n \n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$"}]}
